#
# Rebuild the registered modules for the kernel the NEXT boot will use, while this one still runs and
# the network it needs is still up. The ordinary Update button reaches no upgrade hook - it puts the
# new kernel in /boot and reboots - but rc.reboot, rc.halt and rc.shutdown all run `rc.syshook stop`
# before anything is torn down, so this is the moment that always comes (os-xgs-npu, 2026-10-02).
# Numbered below 80-freebsd, which takes the network with it.
#
# Bounded: a fetch that will not finish must not hold the machine. And never the reason a reboot does
# not happen. With nothing registered, or nothing changed, it is a directory listing and two reads.
: ${FOLLOW:=/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/kernelfollow/follow.sh}
: ${LIMIT:=600}
[ -f "${FOLLOW}" ] || exit 0
/usr/bin/timeout -k 10 "${LIMIT}" /bin/sh "${FOLLOW}" > /dev/null 2>&1
rc=$?
if [ ${rc} -eq 124 ] || [ ${rc} -eq 137 ]; then
	# Since 2026-10-30 follow.sh arms the fall-back before it starts a rebuild, so a run cut off here
	# leaves the next boot on kernel.old for a module the network needs, where it matches that kernel.
	/usr/bin/logger -t kernelfollow -p daemon.err \
	    "at shutdown: a module was not rebuilt in ${LIMIT}s; one the network needs takes kernel.old" \
	    "at the next boot where it matches it, and the rebuild goes on from there"
elif [ ${rc} -ne 0 ]; then
	/usr/bin/logger -t kernelfollow -p daemon.err \
	    "at shutdown: a module could not be rebuilt; see the entries above"
fi
exit 0
